  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The voltage across an inductor is</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mi>L</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p>So the voltage across the inductor will always be opposite to the rate of change of current.</p><p>When&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#62;</mo><mn>0</mn></math>&nbsp;then&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mn>0</mn></math>&nbsp;and vice versa</p><p>Also, the magnitude of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi></math>&nbsp;is proportional to the magnitude of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>.</p><p>When&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>decreases,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi></math>&nbsp;also decreases</p>",
    "image": null
  },
